4. Architectural Principles
4.1 Relation-Driven Architecture

Foresy follows the following mandatory architecture rule:

No business entity directly owns a foreign key to another business entity.

Relationships between business domains are represented through explicit relationship tables.

Therefore:

User
  │
  │
  ▼
UserCompany
  │
  │
  ▼
Company

UserCompany is the explicit relation between User and Company.

The relation table itself may, and must, contain foreign keys to the entities it relates.

The architectural prohibition concerns business entities directly referencing other business entities, not the relationship table.

5. Domain Concepts

FC-08 introduces or formalizes:

User
Company
UserCompany
UserCompany role
Company lifecycle
Company legal context

These concepts remain distinct.

User
  ≠
Company
  ≠
UserCompany
  ≠
Role

A User is a person/account.

A Company represents a legal/business organization.

A UserCompany represents the fact that a User acts in a particular role in relation to a Company.

6. Company

A Company represents a business/legal organization known to Foresy.

A Company is independent from the User who interacts with it.

A Company therefore:

does not contain user_id;
may exist independently from any particular User;
may be associated with multiple Users;
may have multiple UserCompany relationships.
7. Company and Independent

The concept of Independent remains a domain role/context.

It is not equivalent to Company.

Conceptually:

User
  │
  └── UserCompany(role = independent)
             │
             ▼
          Company

A User becomes an Independent in the context of a Company through the UserCompany relationship.

This prevents the architecture from incorrectly embedding "Independent" into the User entity.

8. User Roles

FC-08 initially supports two contextual roles:

independent
client
independent

The User acts on behalf of the Company as an independent professional.

client

The User acts on behalf of the Company as a client/project owner engaging another professional.

The role belongs to the UserCompany relationship, not to User globally.

9. Contextual Roles

A User does not have one immutable global business role.

Instead:

User + Company + Role

defines the user's business context.

Example:

User A
 ├── Company A → independent
 └── Company B → client

This is valid.

10. Multiple Roles for One Company

A User may simultaneously hold multiple roles for the same Company.

Example:

User A
 ├── Company A → independent
 └── Company A → client

This is valid.

The architecture must therefore not enforce:

UNIQUE(user_id, company_id)

Instead, uniqueness applies to:

UNIQUE(user_id, company_id, role)

This allows distinct contextual relationships.

11. Why Roles Must Remain Separate

A User acting as:

independent

and the same User acting as:

client

may generate fundamentally different business activity.

The associated:

mission;
contractual relationship;
activity;
CRA;
financial consequences;

may differ.

FC-08 therefore deliberately does not attempt to collapse those contexts into a single "activity".

Future contracts will determine how those contextual activities are represented.

12. Company Lifecycle

Company lifecycle is represented through soft deletion.

The canonical lifecycle mechanism is:

deleted_at

No status or archived_at field is introduced by FC-08.

A Company is:

active   => deleted_at IS NULL
deleted  => deleted_at IS NOT NULL

The existing project convention must be preserved.

13. No Implicit Re-activation

A deleted Company must not be implicitly reactivated by:

POST /companies;
creating a new UserCompany;
updating an existing relationship.

Reactivation, if required in the future, must be an explicit domain operation.

FC-08 does not introduce reactivation.

14. Simulation Without a Company

A User must be able to use Foresy before having an active Company.

However:

Foresy must not create a fictitious Company merely to support simulation.

There is no:

Company(type = demo)

or:

Company(fake = true)

in FC-08.

Simulation data is conceptually distinct from real Company data.

15. User Without a Company

A newly registered User may have:

0 Company

This is valid.

The onboarding process must therefore not require a Company to exist before the User can access Foresy.

This supports the use case:

User registers
      ↓
No active Company
      ↓
Simulation / exploration
      ↓
Company creation
      ↓
Real activity
16. Company in the Process of Registration

FC-08 does not introduce a fictitious Company to represent a future legal entity.

If the product later needs to represent:

Company being incorporated

that lifecycle must be modeled explicitly in a future contract.

FC-08 does not invent a temporary Company identity.

17. Realized Activity and Forecast

FC-08 does not redefine the CRA.

FC-07 remains the authority for CRA behavior.

In particular, FC-08 does not introduce:

