24. UserCompany Foreign Keys

The relation table contains:

user_id → users.id
company_id → companies.id

This is explicitly allowed by the Relation-Driven architecture.

The restriction is:

Company → User
User → Company

being directly represented by business-entity foreign keys.

The relation table is precisely where those references belong.

25. UserCompany Role

The existing PostgreSQL enum:

user_company_role_enum

is retained.

Supported values for FC-08:

independent
client

No additional role is introduced.

Future roles may be added through a future Feature Contract.

26. UserCompany Uniqueness

The database must enforce:

UNIQUE(user_id, company_id, role)

Therefore:

Valid
User A + Company A + independent
User A + Company A + client
Invalid duplicate
User A + Company A + independent
User A + Company A + independent
27. Soft Delete of UserCompany

UserCompany.deleted_at is introduced.

This allows the historical relationship to be retained.

The active relationship is:

deleted_at IS NULL

The deleted relationship is:

deleted_at IS NOT NULL

A deleted relationship must not appear in ordinary active relationship listings.

28. UserCompany Re-activation

FC-08 does not support implicit reactivation.

Creating a new relationship must not silently revive a deleted relationship.

A future explicit reactivation operation may be introduced separately.

29. Relationship History

The UserCompany relationship itself provides a durable record of contextual association.

This is important for auditability.

Future features may attach domain activity to the appropriate contextual relationship without requiring User or Company to contain role-specific state.

