35. Company Listing
GET /companies

returns the Companies accessible to the authenticated User.

Deleted Companies are excluded from the normal active listing.

The implementation must not rely on default_scope.

36. Company Creation

The primary onboarding path is:

POST /companies

with a Company payload and a contextual role.

Example:

{
  "company": {
    "name": "Example Consulting",
    "siren": "123456789",
    "siret": "12345678900012",
    "legal_form": "EI",
    "vat_regime": "franchise",
    "country": "FR",
    "currency": "EUR"
  },
  "role": "independent"
}
37. Atomic Company Creation

Creating a Company through the onboarding endpoint must atomically create:

Company
+
UserCompany

The operation must occur within one database transaction.

If either operation fails:

Company is not persisted
UserCompany is not persisted

There must be no orphan Company resulting from a failed onboarding operation.

38. Additional UserCompany Creation

When a Company already exists, a User may create an additional contextual relationship through:

POST /user_companies

Example:

{
  "company_id": "uuid",
  "role": "client"
}

This allows:

Company A
 ├── User A → independent
 └── User A → client

without duplicating the Company entity.

39. UserCompany PATCH
PATCH /user_companies/:id

may modify:

role

only.

It must not permit modification of:

user_id
company_id
deleted_at

through ordinary role update semantics.

Changing:

independent → client

is an explicit contextual role change.

If historical preservation of the previous role is required by a future domain contract, that contract must define the required history model.

40. UserCompany DELETE
DELETE /user_companies/:id

performs a soft delete:

deleted_at = current timestamp

The relationship remains persisted.

41. Company DELETE
DELETE /companies/:id

performs a soft delete.

Physical deletion is not performed by FC-08.

The treatment of associated UserCompany records must preserve historical integrity.

No cascading physical deletion may be introduced.

42. Parameter Wrapping

The CompaniesController must explicitly support Rails parameter wrapping.

The controller must use the project's established convention equivalent to:

wrap_parameters :company, include: [...]

The exact included Company attributes must correspond to the FC-08 permitted Company attributes.

role is not a Company attribute.

Therefore:

role

must remain outside the Company wrapper.

Example:

{
  "company": {
    "name": "Example Consulting",
    "siren": "123456789"
  },
  "role": "independent"
}

The controller reads:

params[:company]
params[:role]

independently.

43. Flat JSON Compatibility

The API must correctly handle the established Rails parameter-wrapping behavior.

A flat request such as:

{
  "name": "Example Consulting",
  "siren": "123456789",
  "role": "independent"
}

must be correctly interpreted according to the configured wrap_parameters behavior.

role must remain distinct from Company attributes.

