46. Database Constraints

The database is the final authority for structural integrity.

Required constraints include:

PRIMARY KEY companies.id
PRIMARY KEY user_companies.id

UNIQUE companies.siren
UNIQUE companies.siret

UNIQUE user_companies(user_id, company_id, role)

Foreign keys:

user_companies.user_id → users.id
user_companies.company_id → companies.id
47. Migration Plan

Before applying migrations, implementation must inspect the actual current schema.

The migration sequence is:

47.1 SIREN

Change:

companies.siren

from nullable to non-null.

Add:

UNIQUE INDEX companies.siren

Existing data must be verified before applying the constraint.

47.2 SIRET

Change:

companies.siret

from:

NOT NULL

to:

NULL

Retain its unique index.

PostgreSQL allows multiple NULL values under a standard unique index.

47.3 Legal Form

Ensure:

companies.legal_form

remains nullable.

No destructive rename is performed.

47.4 VAT Regime

Add:

companies.vat_regime

as:

string

nullable.

47.5 UserCompany Soft Delete

Add:

user_companies.deleted_at

as nullable datetime.

47.6 Constraints

Ensure:

UNIQUE(companies.siren)
UNIQUE(companies.siret)
UNIQUE(user_companies.user_id, user_companies.company_id, user_companies.role)

are enforced at database level.

48. Migration Compatibility

The implementation must first inspect:

db/schema.rb

and the actual migrations.

The contract must be implemented against the actual schema, not against assumptions.

Existing UUID primary keys must be retained.

No conversion to bigint is permitted.

